<Base title="How App Composer works" description="Understand the supported agent-operated workflow, independent app contracts, ownership, configuration, updates and safe rollback."><h1>How App Composer works</h1><p class="lede">App Composer prepares a real environment and joins independently maintained apps. It uses external Codex for execution and EmDash for its managed catalogue; it does not host a competing agent.</p><p><a href="/ready/guide">First time? Follow the step-by-step setup guide</a></p><p><a href="/architecture">Read the original visual architecture explainers</a></p><section class="section"><h2>From no idea to a live app</h2><ol><li><strong>Get Ready.</strong> The agent checks its actual workspace, GitHub and Cloudflare tools. It verifies the target and deploys the configurable Hello World reference over HTTPS.</li><li><strong>Init.</strong> Choose a useful preset and customise real fields. Export a versioned spec and a matching prompt, without credentials.</li><li><strong>Compose.</strong> Pin independent source commits, validate their declared routes, data versions and capabilities, and connect their real item/action interface.</li><li><strong>Verify.</strong> Check source identity and live interaction, not just HTTP 200 or a green build.</li></ol></section><section class="section" id="standalone-get-ready"><h2>Get Ready works on its own.</h2><p>The same reusable core powers Composer’s readiness command and an independent Node consumer. It checks the actual agent, GitHub access, Cloudflare account and HTTPS source, saves private progress and rechecks it on resume. A provider interruption stays with the agent for retry; confirmed authentication or permission problems identify the human step.</p><p>The independent consumer has redeployed the reviewed Hello reference and verified its exact source on the existing custom domain. Deployment is an explicit operation with fresh prerequisite, source, account and route checks. The deployment operation cannot run an arbitrary repository or create cloud infrastructure.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/tree/main/lib/get-ready">Read the MIT core</a> · <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/blob/main/examples/get-ready.mjs">Use the standalone example</a></p><p class="note">An explicit clone operation can prepare the maintained Hello source in a new directory from an authorised Codex folder, including one without a Git repository. It checks active tools and a fresh GitHub source read before creating the destination. It preserves existing files and verifies the exact checkout. Your agent must then check that destination itself; cloning grants no upstream push permission. A separate explicit ownership operation can create a new personal private GitHub repository from the unchanged starter, verify its source and rebind the checkout. It refuses existing names and requires a new workspace check. The same installer can prepare local tools in an unchanged personal private copy after checking actual ownership, permission and source. It excludes owner registry files/hooks and preserves an existing partial installation. A separate prepare-reference-target operation checks the existing Cloudflare account, its workers.dev subdomain and an unused Worker name, then prepares only the owned starter’s local deployment configuration. The name is not reserved; no cloud resource is created. After reviewing, committing and pushing that change, the agent can explicitly deploy the owned starter to the unused address. It rechecks private ownership, write permission and current source, builds a temporary snapshot without ignored local files, then verifies routing and the exact HTTPS source. An occupied or partially created Worker is preserved for inspection; there is no automatic retry or overwrite. This supports the prepared configuration only, with all other source bytes matching the maintained starter. Owned Init supports the title/introduction/theme on that verified workers.dev starter. A separate explicit update requires its previous source and currently active single Worker version; both are rechecked before upload and the new live source/version is verified. General source updates and cleanup remain unfinished; the maintained-source release path stays separate. The supported Mac path can install missing pinned dependencies in that reviewed checkout, verify its local Wrangler and resume deployment. Local tool preparation does not require upstream push permission. Deployment keeps its GitHub write and target checks; successful preparation alone does not make the checkout ready. A repeat leaves working tools alone. Global tool installation, initial account/domain setup and fresh-user provisioning remain work to prove. The local programmatic boundary is available; a hosted API and Get Ready MCP server are not exposed yet. Composer and the dashboard extension currently hand execution to your authorised agent.</p></section><section class="section" id="workspace-tools"><h2>Missing tools have a supported next step.</h2><p>On the supported Apple Silicon Mac with active Codex, Node 22 and Git, your agent can run <code>node scripts/ready.mjs --workspace PATH --install-workspace-tools</code>. Usable existing tools are reused. Missing GitHub CLI or pnpm gets a verified private pair inside that folder, including pnpm’s runtime and licences. No machine installation or shell profile is changed.</p><p>The agent selects <code>--use-workspace-tools</code> for later checks in that folder, or <code>--tools-workspace PATH</code> to reuse the pair from a new starter checkout. Each selection checks the complete installed files before execution. An incomplete or changed folder is preserved for inspection. A normal resume never installs, switches tools or retries an operation automatically.</p><p>Preparation grants no account or deployment access. Git and Node installation, other operating systems, first accounts and domain setup remain separate work. <a class="underline" href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer#prepare-missing-workspace-tools">Commands and reviewed distribution sources</a>.</p></section><section class="section" id="app-settings"><h2>Give each app its own settings.</h2><p>In Init, open <strong>Settings for each app</strong>. The reviewed Guestbook and Showcase contracts provide the fields, choices and limits. Enable a separate group to change that app’s title, introduction, theme and layout. Uncheck it to use the shared values.</p><p>The preview and exported version 2 spec resolve the same settings. Older version 1 drafts keep their shared values when reopened; changed source contracts require a review instead of silently changing their meaning. Each separate group contains all supported fields.</p><p>For the existing verified operator pair, <code>pnpm compose --spec PATH --apply-settings</code> verifies the source and owner connection, applies only settings, and confirms the response. It preserves collections and source pins. The agent then checks the standalone and composed views. New customer targets and a general field builder remain work to complete.</p></section><section class="section" id="hello-init"><h2>Turn the reference into a first app.</h2><p>Choose Hello in Init, enter a title and introduction, choose a theme and export the specification. Your agent uses <code>pnpm init:hello</code> to inspect the proposed configuration, then explicitly applies it after the same Get Ready checks. A personal private starter uses its explicit repository and already verified workers.dev address; temporary-address intent creates no new target.</p><p>The command prepares only the local configuration. Your agent reviews the diff, commits and pushes through the independent repository’s release path, deploys and verifies the changed page. A failed readiness check leaves the configuration untouched.</p><p class="note">The maintained authored Hello path uses its existing authorised hostname. The supported private starter accepts only these three fields and the prepared workers.dev profile; its update checks the actual previous source and active Worker version. First accounts, new preview provisioning, arbitrary fork source and cleanup remain separate work. Gallery layout does not apply to Hello.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>The first composition</h2><p>Photo Guestbook owns submitted media, moderation and eligibility. Curated Showcase owns its card collection. An approved item can be selected and featured; opening its card opens the source contribution. Unavailable, hidden or deleted sources fail closed. Composer joins the routes through Worker service bindings; there are no iframes or private media copies.</p><p><a href="/catalogue">Browse the EmDash catalogue</a> · <a href="/demo">Use the live pair</a></p></section><section class="section"><h2>Updates preserve your work.</h2><p>App configuration and owner data live separately from upstream source. A candidate must keep the supported contract and data version, pass capability checks and use exact commits. Incompatible versions or new permissions are rejected before activation. Your working release stays live.</p><p>The first path uses explicit, validated promotion through repository commands. Automatic branch following and webhook-driven updates remain unshipped until their concurrency and conflict gates are proven.</p><p>Code rollback restores an executable only. It never restores an old database, republishes a hidden image or discards later submissions. Storage exports and backups have their own authorised recovery path.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>The supported boundary</h2><p>This first release is operated by an authorised agent using its own GitHub/Cloudflare runtime. Public users can browse examples, explore decisions and export a spec. They cannot launch arbitrary builds, access production credentials or moderate another owner’s collection.</p><p>Owner uploads are capped and invited. No current feature implies a public multi-tenant security guarantee, provider partnership or commercial agreement.</p><p><a href="/state">Try fictional encrypted assistant state</a> · <a href="/extension">Optional Cloudflare dashboard extension</a> · <a href="/release">Verified release evidence</a></p></section><section class="section" id="state-vision"><h2>The portable identity concept.</h2><p>Portable AI State is an independent assistant-context app. This diagram carries the useful idea from the original concept into the current local encryption and disclosure flow.</p><JourneyDiagram kind="state"/><p>You keep the passphrase, reopen the saved export and explicitly choose which memories to copy to a provider.</p><p class="note">Server sync, real private data, automated provider connections and key recovery are not verified. Assistant context and a shared game login are separate capabilities.</p><p><a href="https://ai.loftwah.com">Try the fictional example</a> · <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/issues/44">Shared game identity follow-up</a> · <a href="/release">Current verified boundary</a></p></section><section class="section"><h2>Check a page without collecting its contents.</h2><p>Add <code>?vitals=1</code> to an app’s home page and open “Page performance”. It shows loading, interaction delay and layout shift for that load, using the self-hosted Web Vitals library. Use the app, then read the updated values. They stay in the browser, including on Portable AI State.</p><p>The developer console links to public source and app activity. Each app also has <code>/healthz</code>: a small liveness check with its deployed source revision, not a promise that storage and every user flow work. <a href="/activity">The app leaderboard</a> counts public opens and selected completed actions separately.</p><p>Cloudflare’s existing public-page analytics is a separate operator view. A single local reading does not establish the 75th-percentile field result across phone and desktop visitors. <a href="https://web.dev/articles/vitals">Read the current Web Vitals guidance</a>.</p></section></Base>
